00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:10,000
So in this edition of the Scoutmaster podcast, in Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less,

110
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,000
we're going to talk about discipline and punishment in scouting.

111
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
What's your role as an adult volunteer?

112
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
And that's it's an important message.

113
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:24,000
It's one that we've talked about, not in the not too distant past,

114
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,000
but well, you'll learn a little bit more about why I'm talking about it again

115
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,000
in Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

116
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,000
Then we've got an email question to answer for you

117
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,000
and that's going to tie it up for this edition of the Scoutmaster podcast.

118
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
So let's get started, shall we?

119
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:44,000
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

120
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:55,000
This feature of the podcast, Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less,

121
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:02,000
was intended to be a quick seven minute, five minute talk about some aspect of scouting,

122
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,000
and it never really has been.

123
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
It's always been more than seven minutes,

124
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:10,000
but I'm working at keeping this one under seven minutes, and here's why

125
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
This subject comes up very often.

126
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
I got an email from somebody this week dealing with it

127
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:21,000
and I'm not sharing it here for obvious reasons, because it's a personal matter within a troop-

128
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:25,000
and I actually spoke to them on the phone and I want to let you know.

129
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:30,000
if you ever run into a problem or a brick wall, I will give you a call

130
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:34,000
and all you need to do is touch base with me with an email.

131
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:36,000
We can't figure stuff out in an email.

132
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,000
I would be more than happy to talk to you, give you a call,

133
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
because sometimes we run into serious problems out there

134
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:46,000
and I like to think I know at least how to tell you not to feel so bad,

135
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:52,000
because we all are going to eventually run into some kind of discipline

136
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
or behavior problem with our Scouts.

137
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:57,000
Here's the deal. okay,

138
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Handling these kind of incidents, even though they're upsetting,

139
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
is simpler than you think.

140
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:07,000
How do we handle incidents of bad behavior or bullying or something like that?

141
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:13,000
Well, the basic idea is that you as a Scouting Volunteer,

142
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:18,000
you as a Scout Master, Assistant Scout Master, Committee Member, Cub Master,

143
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,000
you're not there to be a disciplinarian,

144
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:25,000
You're not there to meet out punishment to Scouts.

145
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
That's the job of their parents.

146
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,000
We do Scouting in cooperation with the family

147
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
and in cooperation with a Scout's parents.

148
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:36,000
And if a Scout behaves badly we refer them to their parents

149
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:40,000
for whatever correction or punishment their parents deem necessary.

150
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
Now there are consequences involved during a Scout activity.

151
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
if somebody misbehaves-

152
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:48,000
First, you know they stop the misbehavior.

153
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:52,000
Second, the nature of the misbehavior may kind of disqualify them

154
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,000
for participating in the activity that they're participating in,

155
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:59,000
probably have to step away from that and sit down somewhere and wait,

156
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,000
you know, more or less time out.

157
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:07,000
But the event that you have a behavior problem that you can't correct

158
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,000
just as a matter of course or that you consider is serious enough

159
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
that it needs to be dealt with,

160
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:16,000
we're going to take the next available opportunity with a Scout and his parents.

161
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:19,000
Now if it's a meeting and parents are probably coming to pick the boy up,

162
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
we need to talk to them. then

163
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
If we've been on an outing and there's been some kind of a problem

164
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
and the parents are coming to pick the boy up,

165
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
we need to speak with them. then

166
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
These are face-to-face meetings.

167
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:34,000
We don't communicate these types of things through emails or anything like that.

168
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:37,000
We talk with the Scout and his parent. we explain the situation

169
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:41,000
and we tell the parent that this kind of behavior won't be tolerated in Scouts

170
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,000
and they need to take whatever corrective actions they need to take

171
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:48,000
to assure us that we're not going to be dealing with it anymore.

172
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
It's not our place to discipline Scouts by punishing them.

173
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
The only tools in our toolkit are all positive,

174
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
positive reinforcement and things like that.

175
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,000
If a Scout misbehaves, we take them out of the activity.

176
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:01,000
That's the direct consequence of their misbehavior.

177
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
If they make a big giant mess, they're going to clean up the mess.

178
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,000
If an apology is in order, we're going to ask them to apologize.

179
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
The direct consequences of the behavior.

180
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
as far as punishing them by telling them that you know

181
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:15,000
they can't attend a certain number of campouts or meetings

182
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,000
or having some kind of disciplinary counsel or something like that,

183
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
I don't think it's a good idea.

184
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
I think that's what parents are there for

185
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
and I think we take the boy to the parents.

186
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
We say this kind of behavior is unacceptable

187
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
and you need to take care of it,

188
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
and the next time we see him

189
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,000
we need to be assured that this kind of behavior won't be repeated.

190
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,000
Parents are much more effective at this than Scout leaders are.

191
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
Now, this isn't copping out,

192
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:43,000
This isn't pushing your problems over to somebody else.

193
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:47,000
This is putting discipline, accountability

194
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:51,000
and punishment in the hands of the people who ought to have it:

195
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
the family of the Scout.

196
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:57,000
So I'll repeat again: anything beyond the direct consequences of an action

197
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
that we would identify as punishment,

198
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
that's not scouting. that's not something that we do.

199
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:04,000
that's something that the parents need to take care of.

200
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
My final thought about this is:

201
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:10,000
when Scouts misbehave, Scout leaders feel bad.

202
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
They feel as if they haven't done something

203
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
or they haven't supervised properly.

204
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
Listen, when we're talking about a Boy Scout troop,

205
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
we're talking about activities being predicated

206
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
on the idea of mutual trust.

207
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:25,000
We have to trust Scouts to make good decisions.

208
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:29,000
We have to trust Scouts to be able to supervise themselves.

209
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
Yes, we're going to be there.

210
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,000
We're going to be watching women not hear every word that's said

211
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
and see every action that's taken.

212
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
When Scouts breach that trust,

213
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:41,000
that is not necessarily the fault of the Scout leader.

214
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
You're going to feel bad because you're a big hearted person,

215
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
you're volunteering for Scouts.

216
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
If you're wrong, you're going to feel responsible.

217
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
you're going to feel as if you should have done something

218
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
and you didn't do something

219
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
In one wise and this is very counter-intuitive.

220
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
these kind of problems are good.

221
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,000
We've created an atmosphere of safety and trust.

222
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
Boys sometimes will act out and act poorly

223
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
when they're given that amount of trust and latitude.

224
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
The corrective actions that we take

225
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:10,000
are the consequences that flow directly from the act.

226
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
Any punishment or anything like that

227
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
is left up to the parents.

228
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
They're going to have the experience of growing up right.

229
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
There are going to be instances where this advice

230
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
may seem a little simplistic,

231
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
but I think it gives you a base for solid action

232
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
when you encounter behavioral difficulties

233
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
in your Scout troop.

